course: dictionary
lesson: 9618
title: Structures all around us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A structure is something that has been built, or the way its parts are arranged and fit together. A bridge, a tower and a building are all structures. «The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is about 2,300 km long and is the largest structure built by living things.» Here structure means something made of many parts that fit together.
explain: Structure can also mean how something is arranged. The structure of a story is its beginning, middle and end, and the structure of a sentence is how its words fit together. «Builders think about two kinds of force inside a structure» means they think about how the parts push and pull on each other.
explain: Structure can be a verb too. To structure something means to plan and arrange its parts. «She will structure her talk in three parts» means she will plan the talk so it has three clear sections, one after another.
explain: Some colours are not paint at all. «The blue morpho's wings are bright blue on top and brown with eye spots underneath; the blue comes from tiny structures on the scales that reflect blue light, not from pigment.» So a structure can make colour without any colouring stuff inside.
fact: A structure is something that has been built, like a bridge, tower or building.
fact: The structure of something is how its parts are arranged and fit together.
fact: Structure can mean order and organisation, as in the structure of a story: beginning, middle and end.
fact: «The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is about 2,300 km long and is the largest structure built by living things.»
fact: Triangles make structures stronger, so builders often use them in bridges and towers.
fact: Pigment is coloured stuff in the body, like paint. Some colours, such as the shine of peacock feathers, come from tiny structures that reflect light, not from pigment.
exercise: «The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is the largest structure built by living things.» What does structure mean here? | A kind of fish that lives in the sea | *Something built, made of many parts that fit together | A loud noise made by waves | A type of weather that comes in the spring
exercise: «The story had a clear structure: a beginning, a middle and an end.» What does structure mean here? | A bridge over a river | Something that is very old | *How the parts of the story are arranged | A bright colour on a wing
exercise: «Ella will structure her essay in three parts.» What does structure mean here? | To melt it down | To break something into tiny pieces | *To plan and arrange its parts | To paint it in bright colours
test: «The blue morpho's wings are blue, but the blue comes from tiny structures, not pigment.» What makes the blue colour? | Blue ink that seeps out of the wing | Blue pigment, like paint inside the wing | *Tiny structures that reflect blue light | Sunlight shining on brown scales
test: Why do builders use triangles in a structure? | Triangles make the structure lighter and easier to paint | *Triangles make the structure stronger | Triangles are the cheapest shape to buy | Triangles make the structure look more pretty
exam: «Animal colours come in two kinds: pigments, which are coloured stuff like paint, and structures, tiny shapes that bend and scatter light.» Which kind of colour comes from tiny shapes that bend and scatter light? | Paint from the body | Sunlight | Pigment | *Structures
